.TH size "" "Command"
.SH NAME
size \- print size of an object file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsize [\-c] [\-t] [\fIfile ...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
size -- print size of an object file
Usage:	size [ -c ] [ -t ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-c	Print size of common areas
	-t	Print totals for all files
.HE
.B size
prints, for each
.IR file ,
the size in bytes of its text, initialized data, and uninitialized data
(bss) segments, followed by the total size of those segments in both
decimal and octal.
Each
.I file
must be a linked object file (in
.B l.out
format); if none is given,
.B size
examines the file
.BR l.out .
.PP
Options:
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Also add in the total size of the file's common (external, unresolved
at link time) data, taken from its symbol table.
.IP \fB\-t\fR
If more than one
.I file
is given, print a final
.B Total:
line summing the segment sizes (and, with
.BR \-c ,
the common data) across all files.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B strip
.SH NOTES
The size of the common area is computed by scanning the symbol table
for global, referenced symbols and summing their sizes; if
.B \-c
is not given, this scan is skipped.
